---
import ComponentDoc from '../../layouts/ComponentDoc.astro';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';
import Preview from '@esa/docs/Preview.astro';
import ApiTable from '@esa/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import EsaFieldError from '@esa/ecology/esa-field-error.astro';

// Prose only — names, types and defaults are generated from the component
// source by src/data/component-api.ts, so this table cannot drift.
const describe = {
  message: "Resolved error message. Renders nothing when null or empty.",
  icon: "Show the leading alert icon. On by default — without it, an error line and a help line are the same element in the same slot differing only by colour, which is SC 1.4.1.",
  id: "The element id, so a control's aria-describedby can point at this message. Without one the association this component exists for cannot be written.",
};
---
<ComponentDoc name="Field Error" category="Forms" slug="esa-field-error" type=".astro" status="stable"
  summary="A presentational error line that sits under any input. Pass a resolved <code>message</code> string — it renders a danger-colored line (matching esa-form-field's error styling) or nothing when empty.">

  <Section id="preview" title="Preview">
    <Preview code={`<EsaFieldError message="This field is required." />`}>
      <EsaFieldError message="This field is required." />
    </Preview>
  </Section>

  <Section id="empty" title="Empty state">
    <p>With no <code>message</code> the component renders nothing and collapses out of layout, so it never reserves a gap under the input.</p>
    <Preview code={`<EsaFieldError message="" />
<EsaFieldError message={null} />`}>
      <EsaFieldError message="" />
      <EsaFieldError message={null} />
      <span style="color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);">(nothing rendered above)</span>
    </Preview>
  </Section>

  <Section id="icon" title="With icon">
    <p>Set <code>icon</code> for a small leading alert glyph.</p>
    <Preview code={`<EsaFieldError message="Enter a valid email address." icon />`}>
      <EsaFieldError message="Enter a valid email address." icon />
    </Preview>
  </Section>

  <Section id="composition" title="Under an input">
    <p>It is presentational and composable — drop it beneath any control. Your validation layer decides the message.</p>
    <Preview align="start" code={`<label for="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" type="email" value="not-an-email"
       aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="email-error" />
<EsaFieldError id="email-error" message="Enter a valid email address." icon />`}>
      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; max-width: 320px;">
        <label for="fe-email" style="font-weight: 500;">Email</label>
        <input id="fe-email" type="email" value="not-an-email" aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="fe-email-error" style="padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-background-utility-danger, #ef4444); border-radius: 6px;" />
        <EsaFieldError id="fe-email-error" message="Enter a valid email address." icon />
      </div>
    </Preview>
  </Section>

  <Section id="api" title="API">
    <ApiTable slug="esa-field-error" describe={describe} />
  </Section>

  <Section id="accessibility" title="Accessibility">
    <ul class="a11y">
      <li>
        <strong>This does not announce itself.</strong> It is an <code>.astro</code> component
        rendered at build time, and it renders <em>nothing at all</em> when <code>message</code>
        is empty — so there is no element in the DOM for a live region to update. A live region
        has to already exist, and be empty, before the text arrives; one created together with
        its content is routinely not announced. This page previously claimed otherwise.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its scope is therefore <strong>server-rendered errors already present on page load</strong> —
        the message a form comes back with after a failed round trip. For an error that appears
        <em>during</em> a session, use the control's own <code>error-text</code>: those keep a
        persistent empty message node exactly so the region can fire, and expose
        <code>live-error</code> to opt into announcing it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Purely presentational — it does not own the input's <code>aria-invalid</code> or
        <code>aria-describedby</code>. Give it an <code>id</code> and point the control at it,
        as the example above does.
      </li>
      <li>
        The leading icon is decorative (<code>aria-hidden</code>) and the text carries the
        meaning — but the icon is doing real work for sighted users, since colour alone cannot
        distinguish an error line from a hint (SC 1.4.1). Prefer <code>icon</code> on.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

</ComponentDoc>
